Permissive — the toolchain runtime in every Windows executable
--------------------------------------------------------------

  The Windows executables are built with llvm-mingw 20260922 (UCRT;
  https://github.com/mstorsjo/llvm-mingw). Its linker adds parts of the
  toolchain's runtime to every executable, statically:

    The MinGW-w64 runtime (mingw-w64-crt, https://www.mingw-w64.org/): the
      startup objects (crt2.o, crtbegin.o, crtend.o), libmingw32 (CRT
      start-up, TLS support, pseudo-relocations), libmingwex (among others
      __mingw_printf and gdtoa's floating-point conversions) and the
      static wrappers in its UCRT import library. The mingw-w64 project's
      own licence and the BSD, ISC-style and gdtoa notices of the parts it
      took from elsewhere; a binary must carry them. The text, verbatim
      from mingw-w64 (llvm-mingw installs the same file as
      x86_64-w64-mingw32/share/mingw32/COPYING.MinGW-w64-runtime.txt), is
      LICENSES/MinGW-w64-runtime.txt.
    LLVM libunwind, in builds with -DXBOXRECOMP_APU_DSP=ON: the Rust std in
      dsp56300 unwinds through it, and cmake/dsp56300.cmake links it
      statically. In builds with the option OFF, compiler-rt's builtins
      (libclang_rt.builtins) can take the place of the helpers that Rust's
      compiler_builtins provides when it is ON (___chkstk_ms, 128-bit
      division). Both are Apache-2.0 WITH LLVM-exception, and code not yet
      relicensed from before that change is under the Legacy LLVM
      (University of Illinois/NCSA) licence, whose copyright notice a
      binary redistribution must carry. LICENSES/LLVM-LICENSE.txt is
      llvm-mingw 20260922's LICENSE.TXT, verbatim, with both texts.

  The C runtime itself is the system's Universal CRT (api-ms-win-crt-*),
  imported, not linked. The executables are C only: no libc++ or libc++abi
  is linked, and nothing in them uses winpthreads (its own licence,
  COPYING.winpthreads.txt in llvm-mingw, is not part of the runtime text
  above). They import no libc++.dll, libunwind.dll or libwinpthread-1.dll.
  A game that adds C++ or POSIX-thread code changes this list: lld's
  --verbose names every archive member a link loads.


Derived algorithms — credited, independently implemented
--------------------------------------------------------

  src/d3d/d3d8_swizzle.h
    Implements Xbox Z-order (Morton) texture unswizzling. The
    masked-increment technique is Fabian Giesen's, published on his blog;
    xemu's hw/xbox/nv2a/pgraph/swizzle.c is cited in the file as the
    reference that pointed us at it.

  src/d3d/d3d8_combiners.h
    NV2A register-combiner translation. Cites xemu's pgraph combiner
    implementation as a reference for the hardware's behaviour.

  src/kernel/nv2a_vsh_fields.h
    The NV2A vertex-program field table, as (word, shift, bits). Cites
    xemu's hw/xbox/nv2a/pgraph/vsh.c as the reference for the layout.

  src/kernel/nv2a_combiner.h
    NV2A register-combiner field table and CPU evaluator. Cites xemu's
    hw/xbox/nv2a/pgraph/glsl/psh.c as the reference for the encoding and
    the combiner semantics.

  src/kernel/nv2a_backend_common.h
    Cube-map face selection and face stride. Cites xemu's
    hw/xbox/nv2a/pgraph texture code (texture.c) as the reference for the
    face order and the per-face layout.

  Algorithms and hardware behaviour are not themselves copyrightable, and
  these are our own implementations. They are listed here because the debt is
  real and should be visible even where no licence obligation attaches. If
  anyone believes any of these files crosses from "implements the same
  algorithm" into "copies the expression", raise an issue and we will rewrite
  or relicense it — getting the credit right matters more to us than the licence
  label.


Reference material (no code taken)
-----------------------------------

  xemu, Cxbx-Reloaded and xboxdevwiki were used as references for hardware
  behaviour: register offsets, kernel export ordinals, structure layouts.
  Those are functional facts rather than expression, and no code was copied
  for them.


